<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<sample title="HTTP REST request">
	<sdk_desc>
		Implementing a simple asynchronous <i>HTTP</i> server that sets player world coordinates via external <i>REST API</i> POST requests.
	</sdk_desc>
	<desc>
		<brief>
			<para>
				This example demonstrates how to implement an asynchronous HTTP server that sets the player position in response to external REST API POST requests.
			</para>
			<para>
				For demonstration, open the <i>URL</i> displayed on the plate in the scene.
			</para>
		</brief>
		<para>
			The sample uses a lightweight embedded <ui>HTTP</ui> server to handle incoming <ui>POST</ui> requests to the <strong nt="1">/player_pos</strong> endpoint. When accessed, the server reads the <i>x</i> and <i>y</i> parameters from the request and updates the world-space position of the active player accordingly (preserving the original <i>z</i> coordinate).
		</para>
		<para>
			The server runs directly inside the simulation and starts automatically with the sample. The <ui>URL</ui> is dynamically generated based on the current <ui>IP</ui> and port settings.
		</para>
		<para>This sample can serve as a foundation for interactive control, integration with third-party systems, or any use case where external tools need to influence simulation state at runtime.</para>
	</desc>
	<tags>
		<tag>Network</tag>
	</tags>
</sample>